<%#
  Not a chart wall — the same restraint the landing page states. Numbers that
  drive a decision, each shown with enough of its working that somebody can
  argue with it. Currency is never summed across.
%>
<header class="main-content__header">
  <h1 class="main-content__page-title">Reports — last <%= @days %> days</h1>
  <p class="karwan-report__note">
    Days are <strong>Kabul days</strong>, not the server's. Counts are of <strong>completed</strong>
    work — delivered orders and completed rides; cancelled and failed jobs are excluded except where
    they are the subject. Money is grouped by currency and never added across currencies.
  </p>
</header>

<section class="main-content__body">

  <h2>What we earned</h2>
  <%# TWO NUMBERS, NEVER ONE. Gross value is what passed through our hands;
      commission is what is ours. Under Model A the customer pays the courier
      and the courier pays the merchant, so reading the first as income would
      overstate this business by roughly twenty times. %>
  <table class="karwan-report">
    <thead><tr><th>Currency</th><th>Gross order value <small>(what customers paid)</small></th><th>Commission <small>(ours)</small></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <% (@gross_value.keys | @commission.keys).sort.each do |currency| %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= currency %></td>
          <td><%= number_with_delimiter(@gross_value[currency].to_i) %></td>
          <td><strong><%= number_with_delimiter(@commission[currency].to_i) %></strong></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
      <% if (@gross_value.keys | @commission.keys).empty? %>
        <tr><td colspan="3">Nothing completed in this period.</td></tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Rider utilisation</h2>
  <%# The numerator and the denominator, not only the ratio. A courier told
      only the variance cannot check it, and neither can an owner told only
      "1.8 per rider". %>
  <p class="karwan-report__headline">
    <% if @utilisation[:per_courier_per_day] %>
      <strong><%= @utilisation[:per_courier_per_day] %></strong> jobs per rider per day
      — <%= @utilisation[:jobs] %> jobs ÷ <%= @utilisation[:couriers] %> riders ÷ <%= @utilisation[:days] %> days
    <% else %>
      No rider completed a job in this period.
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <p class="karwan-report__note">
    The denominator is riders who <em>completed at least one job</em> in the period, not riders on
    the books. Forty sign-ups and six working riders is six riders' worth of capacity; dividing by
    forty would report a crisis that is really a sign-up list.
  </p>
  <%# The same jobs over the honest denominator: everyone who made themselves
      available, including those who took nothing. That difference IS the idle
      capacity, and it is the number a hiring decision turns on. %>
  <% if @utilisation[:per_available_per_day] && @utilisation[:history_covers_window] %>
    <p class="karwan-report__headline">
      <strong><%= @utilisation[:per_available_per_day] %></strong> jobs per <em>available</em> rider per day
      — <%= @utilisation[:jobs] %> jobs ÷ <%= @utilisation[:available_couriers] %> riders who went on shift
      ÷ <%= @utilisation[:days] %> days
    </p>
    <p class="karwan-report__note">
      This is the one to decide recruitment on. The figure above counts only riders who completed
      work; this one counts everyone who made themselves available, so the gap between the two
      <strong>is</strong> your idle capacity.
    </p>
  <% elsif @utilisation[:history_from].present? %>
    <p class="karwan-report__note karwan-report__note--warn">
      <strong>Shift history does not cover this whole window.</strong> Recording began
      <%= @utilisation[:history_from].strftime("%d %b %Y") %>, so the figure above still counts only
      riders who completed work and therefore <strong>flatters the business</strong> — idle capacity
      is invisible in it. Dividing by an incomplete shift history would report a crisis instead, so
      it is not shown. Both become available once the window sits inside the recorded period.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <p class="karwan-report__note karwan-report__note--warn">
      <strong>This figure flatters the business.</strong> A rider who was available all day and took
      nothing is not counted, so idle capacity is invisible. Shift history has just been added and
      has recorded nothing yet — it <strong>cannot be backfilled</strong>, so this becomes answerable
      only for days from here on. <strong>Do not use this alone to decide whether to recruit.</strong>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <h2>Orders per day</h2>
  <%# Placed AND delivered. Placed alone hides the day when everything was
      ordered and nothing arrived, which is the day worth seeing. %>
  <table class="karwan-report">
    <thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Placed</th><th>Delivered</th><th>Rides</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <% @per_day.each do |row| %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= row[:date].strftime("%a %d %b") %></td>
          <td><%= row[:placed] %></td>
          <td><%= row[:delivered] %></td>
          <td><%= row[:rides] %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Demand we could not serve — same <%= @days %> days</h2>
  <%# THE OTHER HALF OF UTILISATION, and the half that cannot flatter.

      The utilisation figure above divides by couriers who FINISHED a job, so
      idle capacity is invisible and the number reads better than the truth.
      This is the same shortage from the demand side: every one of these is a
      person who asked and did not get anybody, and there is no way to count it
      that makes it look better than it is.

      Kept apart from the cancellations below on purpose. A passenger changing
      their mind is normal and not actionable; nobody being available is the one
      that means hire somebody. Averaged into "cancellations" they hide each
      other. %>
  <table class="karwan-report">
    <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Deliveries</th><th>Rides</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Nobody available to take it</strong></td>
        <td><strong><%= @lost[:unserved][:deliveries] %></strong></td>
        <td><strong><%= @lost[:unserved][:rides] %></strong></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="karwan-report__note">
    <% unserved = @lost[:unserved][:deliveries] + @lost[:unserved][:rides] %>
    <% if unserved.positive? %>
      <strong><%= unserved %></strong> job<%= "s" unless unserved == 1 %> asked for and not served.
      Read this next to the utilisation figure above, not instead of it: that one counts the couriers who
      worked, this counts the demand they could not reach.
    <% else %>
      Every job asked for in this window found a courier.
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <h2>Cancellations — same <%= @days %> days</h2>
  <%# Ranked, and split by demand type rather than summed: a reason belongs to
      one enum or the other, and `passenger_changed_mind` is not
      `customer_changed_mind`. Adding them would invent a word neither list
      contains. Reasons that did not occur are omitted, as elsewhere on this
      page — except the shortage above, which is shown at zero because a zero
      there is the thing being watched. %>
  <table class="karwan-report">
    <thead><tr><th>Reason</th><th>Deliveries</th><th>Rides</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <% @lost[:by_reason].each do |reason, split| %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= reason.to_s.humanize %></td>
          <td><%= split[:deliveries] %></td>
          <td><%= split[:rides] %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
      <% if @lost[:by_reason].empty? %>
        <tr><td colspan="3">No cancellation carried a reason in this window.</td></tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="karwan-report__note">
    <%= @lost[:total] %> cancelled in total, including those with no reason on record.
  </p>

  <h2>Shops to ring — last 30 days</h2>
  <%# THE ONE QUESTION THIS PAGE COULD NOT ANSWER. Everything above is
      platform-wide and says WHY orders fail. TRUST_AND_REPUTATION.md §5-B asks
      WHICH SHOP: "a restaurant cancelling a fifth of its orders is visible
      before its customers leave". With ten restaurants he knows personally,
      the name is the actionable part and the rate is the sentence.

      THE DENOMINATOR IS ON EVERY ROW. "40%" alone is unarguable; "4 of 10" is
      something an owner can weigh against a shop he knows. Same rule as the
      settlement screen showing expected AND counted.

      NO PENALTY IS IMPLIED. §5-C is explicit that a cancellation carries no
      consequence in v0 — "visibility is not a penalty". This is a list of
      conversations to have, not a list of shops to punish. %>
  <% if @unreliable.any? %>
    <table class="karwan-report">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Shop</th><th>Lost</th><th>Refused</th>
          <th>Never answered</th><th>Cancelled after accepting</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% @unreliable.each do |row| %>
          <% shop = @unreliable_shops[row[:merchant_id]] %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= link_to shop&.name || "merchant ##{row[:merchant_id]}", admin_merchant_path(row[:merchant_id]) %></td>
            <td>
              <strong><%= number_to_percentage(row[:unfulfilled_rate], precision: 1) %></strong>
              <small>(<%= row[:unfulfilled] %> of <%= row[:orders] %> orders)</small>
            </td>
            <td><%= row[:refused] %></td>
            <td>
              <%= row[:never_answered] %>
              <%# WHICH KIND OF SILENCE. A shop toggled open past its own closing
                  time is orderable into an empty kitchen, and the timeout files
                  the result as "nobody answered" — docs/NOTES.md measured 23 of
                  32 shops in that state at 21:08 one evening. Without this the
                  column sends the owner to talk about a tablet when the problem
                  is a switch. Nothing here blocks an order or moves a toggle;
                  the policy is his and is written up unchanged. %>
              <% stale = row[:unanswered_causes][:outside_posted_hours].to_i %>
              <% unknown = row[:unanswered_causes][:no_hours_posted].to_i %>
              <% if stale.positive? %>
                <small>(<%= stale %> after their posted closing time — a toggle, not a tablet)</small>
              <% elsif unknown.positive? && row[:never_answered] == unknown %>
                <small>(no posted hours, so the cause is not known)</small>
              <% end %>
            </td>
            <td><%= row[:cancelled_after_accepting] %></td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="karwan-report__note">
      A shop that answers and says no is a supply problem; a shop that never
      answers is a tablet nobody is watching. They are different phone calls,
      which is why they are different columns.
      Shops with fewer than <%= Merchants::Reliability::MIN_ORDERS_TO_RANK %>
      orders are left out — one bad evening at a new shop is not a reason to ring it.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <p class="karwan-report__note">
      <%# Shown at zero, like `unserved` above: an absent section reads as "not
          measured", and "every shop delivered what it was sent" is the thing
          being watched. %>
      Every shop with at least <%= Merchants::Reliability::MIN_ORDERS_TO_RANK %>
      orders delivered what it was sent.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <h2>Couriers to ring — last 30 days</h2>
  <%# TRUST_AND_REPUTATION.md §5-A and §5-E, the courier's side of the table
      above. Declining is normal — §5-A: "another rider picks it up, and the
      platform should not penalise it" — so this is a share, with its
      denominator, and not a list of faults. An offer let run out is a minute a
      customer waited; one declined was looked at. Different columns.

      A recurring passenger among rides that ended mid-way is §5-E's fraud
      shape ("they tell the customer to cancel, to keep the commission") and is
      listed whatever the courier's volume. Nothing acts on any of it. %>
  <% if @couriers_to_ring.any? %>
    <table class="karwan-report">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Courier</th><th>Not taken</th><th>Declined</th><th>Let run out</th>
          <th>Rides ended mid-way</th><th>Same passenger again</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% @couriers_to_ring.each do |row| %>
          <% courier = @couriers_to_ring_people[row[:courier_id]] %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= link_to courier&.display_name || "user ##{row[:courier_id]}", admin_user_path(row[:courier_id]) %></td>
            <td>
              <% if row[:not_taken_rate] %>
                <strong><%= number_to_percentage(row[:not_taken_rate], precision: 1) %></strong>
                <small>(<%= row[:declined] + row[:timed_out] %> of <%= row[:offers] %> offers)</small>
              <% else %>
                <small>no offers answered</small>
              <% end %>
            </td>
            <td><%= row[:declined] %></td>
            <td><%= row[:timed_out] %></td>
            <td><%= row[:rides_ended_mid_way] %></td>
            <td>
              <% if row[:repeated_passengers].any? %>
                <%= row[:repeated_passengers].map { |id, n| "#{@couriers_to_ring_people[id]&.display_name || "user ##{id}"} ×#{n}" }.join(", ") %>
              <% else %>
                —
              <% end %>
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="karwan-report__note">
      Couriers with fewer than <%= Couriers::Reliability::MIN_OFFERS_TO_RANK %>
      answered offers are left out of the share, and listed only if a passenger
      recurs among their rides that ended mid-way.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <p class="karwan-report__note">
      Every courier with at least <%= Couriers::Reliability::MIN_OFFERS_TO_RANK %>
      answered offers took all of them, and no passenger recurs among rides that ended mid-way.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <h2>Why shops refused orders — same <%= @days %> days</h2>
  <%# THREE DIFFERENT EVENTS, AND THEY MUST NOT BE ADDED TOGETHER.

      A merchant who tapped "closing" made a decision. A merchant who never
      touched the tablet made none — the timeout job closed the order for him,
      with the same reason written in the same column. Shown as one figure, a
      kitchen that never answers reads as a kitchen that keeps shutting early,
      and the fix for those two is not the same fix.

      The third line is neither, and it exists because the two-line version was
      rendered against the real database and claimed eleven timeouts that left
      no trace of a timeout. An order whose rejection was never recorded is
      counted as itself rather than attributed to a cause. %>
  <table class="karwan-report">
    <thead><tr><th>Reason</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <% @rejections[:by_merchant].each do |reason, count| %>
        <tr><td><%= reason.to_s.humanize %></td><td><%= count %></td></tr>
      <% end %>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Nobody answered</strong> <small>(closed by the timeout, not by a person)</small></td>
        <td><strong><%= @rejections[:never_answered] %></strong></td>
      </tr>
      <% if @rejections[:unrecorded].positive? %>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Not recorded</strong> <small>(rejected with no transition row — who did this is unknown)</small></td>
          <td><strong><%= @rejections[:unrecorded] %></strong></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
      <tr>
        <td>All rejected orders</td>
        <td><%= @rejections[:total] %></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="karwan-report__note">
    <%# The percentage is printed only when there is something to attribute. "0%
        of rejections were closed by the timeout" followed by an explanation of
        what a timeout means is three lines telling the reader about a thing
        that did not happen. %>
    <% if @rejections[:never_answered].positive? %>
      <%= number_to_percentage(@rejections[:never_answered] * 100.0 / @rejections[:total], precision: 0) %>
      of rejections were closed by the timeout without anyone in the shop answering.
      An order counted on that line was not refused — <strong>it was not looked at</strong>, which is a
      staffing or a hardware problem rather than a kitchen one.
    <% elsif @rejections[:total].zero? %>
      No orders were rejected in this window.
    <% end %>
    A rejection a merchant made without a reason on record appears only in the total.
    <% if @rejections[:unrecorded].positive? %>
      <strong>The "not recorded" line is a defect, not a business figure.</strong>
      Every rejection is supposed to write a transition with its actor; these did not, so nothing
      can say afterwards who rejected them. It cannot be backfilled.
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <h2>Failure reasons — same <%= @days %> days</h2>
  <%# Ranked, and reasons that never occurred are omitted rather than listed as
      zero — a column of zeroes hides the one that is climbing. %>
  <table class="karwan-report">
    <thead><tr><th>Reason</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <% @failures.each do |reason, count| %>
        <tr><td><%= reason.to_s.humanize %></td><td><%= count %></td></tr>
      <% end %>
      <% if @failures.empty? %>
        <tr><td colspan="2">No failed orders or rides on record.</td></tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Priced by road, or by straight line — last 30 days, and today</h2>
  <%# WHEN THE ROUTER IS DOWN, EVERY FARE IS QUIETLY LOW. The fallback to the
      straight line is automatic and its only other trace is a log line, so
      this count is what shows an outage: straight-line rows appearing while
      routing_distance_source is osrm. "Unrecorded" is a row that never went
      through a quote. See Routing::DistanceSources. %>
  <table class="karwan-report">
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>By road</th><th>By straight line</th><th>Unrecorded</th><th>Straight-line share</th><th>Today: road / straight / unrecorded</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% @distance_sources.counts.each do |kind, spans| %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= kind %></td>
          <td><%= spans[:window]["osrm"] %></td>
          <td><strong><%= spans[:window]["straight_line"] %></strong></td>
          <td><%= spans[:window]["unrecorded"] %></td>
          <td><%= (share = @distance_sources.straight_line_share(kind)) ? "#{share}%" : "—" %></td>
          <td><%= spans[:today].values.join(" / ") %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Roads far longer than the straight line — last 30 days</h2>
  <%# A MEASUREMENT, NOT A RULE. The delivery fee is charged on the road
      distance, and nothing bounds it: across Kabul a road runs a median 1.6x
      the straight line, and a few run 4-7x on what look like gaps in the map
      data. Whether to cap that is the owner's decision — a cap takes money
      off the courier unless the platform pays it — and this shows which of
      his real orders a given ratio would have touched. The fee column is what
      was charged; nothing here re-prices anything. %>
  <%= form_with url: admin_reports_path, method: :get, data: { turbo: false }, class: "karwan-inline-form" do |f| %>
    <%= f.label :detour_ratio, "Road more than" %>
    <%= f.number_field :detour_ratio, value: @detours.ratio, step: 0.1, min: 1 %>
    <span>times the straight line</span>
    <%= f.submit "Show", class: "button" %>
  <% end %>
  <p>
    <strong><%= @detours.over.size %></strong> of <%= @detours.measured.size %> road-priced orders
    since <%= l(@detours.since.to_date) %> ran more than <%= @detours.ratio %>x the straight line.
  </p>
  <% if @detours.over.any? %>
    <table class="karwan-report">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Order</th><th>Straight line</th><th>Road</th><th>Ratio</th><th>Delivery fee charged</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% @detours.over.each do |row| %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= link_to row.order.code, admin_order_path(row.order) %></td>
            <td><%= number_with_precision(row.straight_km, precision: 2) %> km</td>
            <td><%= number_with_precision(row.road_km, precision: 2) %> km</td>
            <td><strong><%= row.ratio %>x</strong></td>
            <td><%= row.order.delivery_fee %> <%= row.order.currency %></td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  <% end %>

</section>
